    "story": "From the side there is nothing to imagine. Every rod, every driver, every rivet stands in line for inspection, the whole machine laid out like an argument she expects to win. Men who build engines will tell you the broadside is the honest view: a locomotive can hide her flaws from the front, dress herself in steam and drama, but from the side she is only ever exactly what she is. Sixteen has never minded the inspection. Stand and count. She'll wait.",
    "notes": "The broadside is railroading's formal portrait, and this one is composed like a builder's plate photograph promoted to art: the locomotive parallel to the picture plane, the camera low, the storm doing the dramatics overhead. Every system of the machine is legible, from pilot to cab, which makes the image a favorite of collectors who come to steam from the mechanical side. What lifts it past documentation is the light, a dark sky pressing down and the iron answering with its own highlights, machine and weather striking the same pose.",
