    "story": "Stand on the rail and the whole yard arranges itself around one decision. She comes out of her own steam dead center, neither veering nor hurrying, and the track does what track has always done: it takes the argument out of it. Left or right is for roads. This is a railroad, and the only direction ever offered was the one already laid in iron before she was built. She accepts it completely. That's not surrender. That's the oldest agreement in the valley, and both sides keep it still.",
    "notes": "The boldest composition in the series and the simplest: one rail, centered, running from the viewer's feet to the engine's pilot, with the yard falling away symmetrically on both sides. The locomotive, wrapped in her own steam, becomes a dark apparition at the vanishing point rather than a machine on display. It is the purest story-frame in the set, an image that argues railroading was never about destinations at all, only about the narrow, decided path between them.",
