    "story": "Same engine, same proud stance as her broadside portrait, but the years have had their say. This is how the old-timers keep her: not in the blacks and whites of the record books, but in the amber of a photograph left too long in a station window, the one everybody in the valley walked past for forty years until it WAS her, as far as they were concerned. Sixteen doesn't mind. An engine that outlives her own century can afford to be remembered in any color the remembering chooses.",
    "notes": "Her Broadside in sepia, brighter and warmer than the black and white master, and presented deliberately as the memory version of the same stance. The amber tonality flattens the contrast into something closer to an albumen print, the kind of image a station agent would have pinned above a desk. As a pair with the master it lets a collector choose between the record and the recollection, and it documents Wayne Heim's working method of returning to a strong frame in more than one emotional register.",
