    "story": "All that way for this: a wooden platform, a station board, and a small crowd's worth of waiting rolled into a few figures with their faces turned toward the sound. She comes in the way arrivals used to matter, with the whole town's afternoon reorganized around her: letters, parcels, a cousin from two valleys over, news that isn't in any paper yet. The plume folds itself away. The brakes sigh. For about ninety seconds, this platform is the center of the world. Then she leaves, and it goes back to being a platform.",
    "notes": "The station scene is the social heart of any railroad series, and this arrival carries it: the platform elevation puts the viewer among the waiting, the station sign anchoring the composition while steam drifts low across the boards. Where the series' other frames isolate the machine, this one restores her context, the building, the crowd implied at the frame's edges, the everyday ceremony of a town receiving its train. It is the warmest image in the set, and the one most likely to start conversations about grandparents.",
